---
import CaseCompanion from '../../case/CaseCompanion.astro';
import CaseStudyBar from '../../case/CaseStudyBar.astro';
import CaseHero from './CaseHero.astro';
import StoryBeat from './StoryBeat.astro';
import CaseNamed from './CaseNamed.astro';
import CaseNext from './CaseNext.astro';

import tripBefore from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/trip-before.png';
import tripAfter from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/trip-after.png';
import routeBefore from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/route-before.png';
import routeAfter from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/route-after.png';
import pricePolicies from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/price-policies.png';
import sightseeingReview from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/sightseeing-review.png';
import luggageCabs from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/luggage-cabs.png';
import luggageInputs from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/luggage-inputs.png';
import luggageFit from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/luggage-fit.png';
import airportListing from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/airport-listing.png';
import airportReview from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/airport-review.png';
import airportTracked from '../../../assets/projects/clarity/airport-tracked.png';

interface NextProject {
  href: string;
  title: string;
}

interface Props {
  year: number;
  role: string;
  next: NextProject | null;
}

const { year, role, next } = Astro.props;
---

<article class="case">
  <CaseStudyBar />
  <CaseCompanion defaultLine="The fare was on screen. The trip was not." />
  <CaseHero
    year={year}
    role={role}
    guideLine="The fare was on screen. The trip was not."
  />

  <section
    class="story-beat case-open"
    aria-labelledby="case-open"
    data-guide="Six decisions were left to be guessed. I took them in that order."
    data-guide-mode="work"
  >
    <h2 class="case-section" id="case-open">The fare was visible. The trip was not.</h2>
    <p class="case-body">
      An outstation cab booking showed a price and left the shape of the trip to be guessed. Round
      trip, the road, what the fare included, sightseeing, the bags, and an airport pickup each hid a
      decision. The six parts below follow that order. Each one opens with the friction, then the
      screen that carries the change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <StoryBeat
    index="01"
    id="trip-type"
    title="One-way versus round trip."
    line="Round trip never said whether the cab stayed. I named what happens."
    eager
    metricLabel="Over one year · outstation"
    screens={[
      {
        src: tripBefore,
        alt: 'Cab search with One Way and Round Trip as unlabeled radio options.',
        caption: 'Round trip was a label. It did not say whether the cab stayed.',
      },
      {
        src: tripAfter,
        alt: 'Search with One-way, Get dropped off, and Round-trip, Keep cab till return.',
        caption: 'The choice now says what happens: dropped off, or the cab stays until return.',
      },
    ]}
    metrics={[
      { value: '54%', label: 'Overall round-trip increase' },
      { value: '8%', label: 'Round-trip share' },
    ]}
  >
    <p>People could not tell what a round trip was promising.</p>
    <p>
      Does it mean drop now, and a pickup later on the return day? Or does the cab stay for the whole
      trip?
    </p>
    <p slot="more" class="story-more">
      Alongside that, multicity was up 82% year on year and one-way 39%. Multicity’s share was 28%.
      One-way’s share was down 3%.
    </p>
  </StoryBeat>

  <StoryBeat
    index="02"
    id="route"
    title="Which route am I paying for?"
    line="The fare did not name the road. That was turning into arguments with the driver."
    screens={[
      {
        src: routeBefore,
        alt: 'Cab list that states 145 km and about 2.5 hours, with no road named.',
        caption: 'Distance and time, and no road. The fare did not say which way the cab would go.',
      },
      {
        src: routeAfter,
        alt: 'The same cab list with Via Fastest Route, Expressway Road, and a link to other routes.',
        caption: 'The list names the road, including the expressway, and offers the other routes.',
      },
    ]}
  >
    <p>
      Without the route, pickup and drop were a fare — and then an argument. There was no clarity
      about the road being paid for, and that turned into on-ground conflicts with the driver.
    </p>
    <p>That theme was 10% of overall submit NPS feedback.</p>
    <p slot="note" class="story-note">Not live. No data yet.</p>
  </StoryBeat>

  <StoryBeat
    index="03"
    id="price"
    title="What am I paying for?"
    line="I put what the fare includes on the review, before price is the only thing left."
    metricLabel="Info clarity"
    screens={[
      {
        src: pricePolicies,
        alt: 'Review screen listing included kilometres, tolls, driver allowance, waiting time, and fuel.',
        caption: 'The review names the inclusions before the fare is the only thing left to debate.',
      },
    ]}
    metrics={[
      { value: '6%', label: 'Conversion increase from review' },
      { value: '10%', label: 'Fewer calls about policies and on-ground issues' },
    ]}
  >
    <p>
      Price shock is a review problem: the fare is on screen, and the reason for it is not. The review
      now leads with the shape of the ride, then the rules that apply to it — one-way policies and
      round-trip policies written apart.
    </p>
  </StoryBeat>

  <StoryBeat
    index="04"
    id="sightseeing"
    title="Sightseeing, in the fare or on top of it."
    line="When the fare jumps, I keep the reason on the same screen."
    metricLabel="Once sightseeing is shown"
    screens={[
      {
        src: sightseeingReview,
        alt: 'Review for Ooty to Mysore to Ooty, with inclusions above a full pay of 4,078 rupees.',
        caption: 'The fare can jump — here to ₹4,078 — with the inclusions still on the same review.',
      },
    ]}
    metrics={[
      { value: '3%', label: 'KM top-up attach, when shown' },
      { value: '4 pp', label: 'Overall conversion increase' },
    ]}
  >
    <p>Sightseeing follows what supply sent. When the fare jumps, the policies that explain it stay on the same screen.</p>
    <p>
      When supply leaves very little buffer, the extra distance is an add-on. When those kilometres are
      already in the base price, suggested spots get more room, so the sightseeing reads as included.
    </p>
  </StoryBeat>

  <StoryBeat
    index="05"
    id="luggage"
    title="Price had become the only decision."
    line="Price had become the only way to choose. I asked who is travelling, and how many bags."
    metricLabel="Luggage"
    screens={[
      {
        src: luggageCabs,
        alt: 'Cab list where each option leads with price, seats, and kilometres.',
        caption: 'Price, car, fuel, kilometres, seats. Nothing said whether the bags would fit.',
      },
      {
        src: luggageInputs,
        alt: 'Search form with a Travellers and bags field under the trip start time.',
        caption: 'Travellers and bags are asked up front, the same kind of input flights and hotels already use.',
      },
      {
        src: luggageFit,
        alt: 'Results with a recommended Innova marked Best Fit for 4 travellers and 4 bags.',
        caption: 'One cab is recommended for the party. Others can add a roof carrier when that is what fits.',
      },
    ]}
    metrics={[
      { value: '4%', label: 'Increase in conversion' },
      { value: '26%', label: 'Collection rate of luggage and passenger info' },
    ]}
  >
    <p>
      On the cab selection screen, each option showed the usual basics. Nothing said whether the car
      could take the people and the bags. Reassurance about fitment follows onto the review, in an
      explainer sheet.
    </p>
    <p slot="more" class="story-more">
      Best fit was shown on 95% of searches, and on 80% of searches for 4–6 passengers. Clicks on those
      best-fit cabs, compared with the rest, were 95% overall and 93% for 4–6 passengers.
    </p>
  </StoryBeat>

  <StoryBeat
    index="06"
    id="airport"
    title="Cancelled before pickup."
    line="Most of those cancellations happened before pickup. The cab now waits on the flight."
    metricLabel="Flight tracking"
    screens={[
      {
        src: airportListing,
        alt: 'Airport cab list with a Flight Tracked Cab note above the cars.',
        caption: 'The list offers a cab that waits on the flight, not a clock set at booking.',
      },
      {
        src: airportReview,
        alt: 'Review with a field to enter a flight number for a flight-tracked cab.',
        caption: 'If MakeMyTrip does not already have the flight, the review asks for it.',
      },
      {
        src: airportTracked,
        alt: 'Review showing IndiGo 6E 2386 and a note that pickup syncs to landing time.',
        caption: 'Once the flight is attached, pickup time follows the landing.',
      },
    ]}
    metrics={[
      { value: '75%', label: 'Attach' },
      { value: '33%', label: 'Reduction in cancellation' },
    ]}
  >
    <p>
      Of airport pickup bookings, 20% went unfulfilled. Of those, 85% were cancelled by the traveller
      before pickup.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hypothesis was a timing mismatch: the flight had moved, and the cab had not. Tracking follows
      the flight. When a MakeMyTrip flight booking already exists, it can be shown. When it doesn’t, the
      review asks for the flight. Tracking stays on after booking.
    </p>
  </StoryBeat>

  <CaseNamed guideLine="The booking says the trip out loud." />
  <CaseNext next={next} />
</article>

<style>
  .case-open {
    margin-top: var(--case-gap);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    max-width: 42ch;
  }
</style>
